I'll solve this using group-theoretic selection rules for the octahedral group O (432).

## Setup

The dipole matrix element $J = \int \phi_1\, x\, \phi_2\, d\tau$ is non-zero only if the integrand $\phi_1\, x\, \phi_2$ contains a component transforming as the totally symmetric representation $\mathrm{A}_1$. Equivalently,

$$\Gamma(\phi_1) \otimes \Gamma(x) \otimes \Gamma(\phi_2) \supseteq \mathrm{A}_1.$$

Since all irreps involved are real, this is equivalent to requiring

$$\Gamma(\phi_2) \in \Gamma(\phi_1) \otimes \Gamma(x), \qquad \Gamma(x) = \mathrm{T}_1.$$

## Character table of O

| Class | $E$ | $8C_3$ | $3C_2$ | $6C_4$ | $6C_2'$ |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| $\mathrm{A}_1$ | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 | 1 |
| $\mathrm{A}_2$ | 1 | 1 | 1 | $-1$ | $-1$ |
| $\mathrm{E}$  | 2 | $-1$ | 2 | 0 | 0 |
| $\mathrm{T}_1$ | 3 | 0 | $-1$ | 1 | $-1$ |
| $\mathrm{T}_2$ | 3 | 0 | $-1$ | $-1$ | 1 |

## Required direct products with $\mathrm{T}_1$

Using the reduction formula $n_i = \tfrac{1}{24}\sum_k |C_k|\,\chi^{(\Gamma)}(C_k)\,\chi^{(i)}(C_k)$:

**$\mathrm{T}_1 \otimes \mathrm{T}_1$** (chars $9,0,1,1,1$):
$$n_{\mathrm{A}_1}=\tfrac{9+0+3+6+6}{24}=1,\; n_{\mathrm{A}_2}=0,\; n_{\mathrm{E}}=\tfrac{18+0+6}{24}=1,\; n_{\mathrm{T}_1}=\tfrac{27-3+6-6}{24}=1,\; n_{\mathrm{T}_2}=\tfrac{27-3-6+6}{24}=1.$$
$$\mathrm{T}_1\otimes\mathrm{T}_1 = \mathrm{A}_1 + \mathrm{E} + \mathrm{T}_1 + \mathrm{T}_2.$$

**$\mathrm{T}_1 \otimes \mathrm{E}$** (chars $6,0,-2,0,0$):
$$n_{\mathrm{T}_1}=\tfrac{18+6}{24}=1,\; n_{\mathrm{T}_2}=\tfrac{18+6}{24}=1,\;\text{others }0.$$
$$\mathrm{T}_1\otimes\mathrm{E} = \mathrm{T}_1 + \mathrm{T}_2.$$

**$\mathrm{T}_1 \otimes \mathrm{A}_1 = \mathrm{T}_1$** (trivial).

**$\mathrm{T}_1 \otimes \mathrm{T}_2$** (chars $9,0,1,-1,-1$):
$$n_{\mathrm{A}_2}=\tfrac{9+3+6+6}{24}=1,\; n_{\mathrm{E}}=1,\; n_{\mathrm{T}_1}=1,\; n_{\mathrm{T}_2}=1,\; n_{\mathrm{A}_1}=0.$$
$$\mathrm{T}_1\otimes\mathrm{T}_2 = \mathrm{A}_2 + \mathrm{E} + \mathrm{T}_1 + \mathrm{T}_2.$$

## Applying to each pair

For each pair we check whether $\Gamma(\phi_1)\otimes\mathrm{T}_1\otimes\Gamma(\phi_2) \supseteq \mathrm{A}_1$, i.e. whether $\Gamma(\phi_2) \in \Gamma(\phi_1)\otimes\mathrm{T}_1$.

**(a)** $\phi_1=\Psi_{d1}\,(E)$, $\phi_2=\Psi_s\,(A_1)$:
$$\mathrm{E}\otimes\mathrm{T}_1 = \mathrm{T}_1+\mathrm{T}_2 \not\ni \mathrm{A}_1.$$
No $\mathrm{A}_1$ component. **$J=0$; cannot be non-zero.**

**(b)** $\phi_1=\Psi_{d1}\,(E)$, $\phi_2=\Psi_{p1}\,(T_1)$:
$$\mathrm{E}\otimes\mathrm{T}_1 = \mathrm{T}_1+\mathrm{T}_2 \ni \mathrm{T}_1.$$
Full check: $\mathrm{E}\otimes\mathrm{T}_1\otimes\mathrm{T}_1 = (\mathrm{T}_1+\mathrm{T}_2)\otimes\mathrm{T}_1 = (\mathrm{A}_1+\mathrm{E}+\mathrm{T}_1+\mathrm{T}_2)+(\mathrm{A}_2+\mathrm{E}+\mathrm{T}_1+\mathrm{T}_2) \supseteq \mathrm{A}_1$. **$J$ can be non-zero.**

**(c)** $\phi_1=\Psi_{d2}\,(E)$, $\phi_2=\Psi_{d1}\,(E)$:
$$\mathrm{E}\otimes\mathrm{T}_1 = \mathrm{T}_1+\mathrm{T}_2 \not\ni \mathrm{E}.$$
Equivalently $\mathrm{E}\otimes\mathrm{T}_1\otimes\mathrm{E}=(\mathrm{T}_1+\mathrm{T}_2)\otimes\mathrm{E}=2\mathrm{T}_1+2\mathrm{T}_2$, no $\mathrm{A}_1$. **$J=0$; cannot be non-zero.**

**(d)** $\phi_1=\Psi_s\,(A_1)$, $\phi_2=\Psi_{d3}\,(T_2)$:
$$\mathrm{A}_1\otimes\mathrm{T}_1 = \mathrm{T}_1 \not\ni \mathrm{T}_2.$$
Equivalently $\mathrm{A}_1\otimes\mathrm{T}_1\otimes\mathrm{T}_2 = \mathrm{T}_1\otimes\mathrm{T}_2 = \mathrm{A}_2+\mathrm{E}+\mathrm{T}_1+\mathrm{T}_2$, no $\mathrm{A}_1$. **$J=0$; cannot be non-zero.**

**(e)** $\phi_1=\Psi_{p1}\,(T_1)$, $\phi_2=\Psi_s\,(A_1)$:
$$\mathrm{T}_1\otimes\mathrm{T}_1 = \mathrm{A}_1+\mathrm{E}+\mathrm{T}_1+\mathrm{T}_2 \ni \mathrm{A}_1.$$
**$J$ can be non-zero.**

## Summary

| Pair | $\Gamma(\phi_1)\otimes\mathrm{T}_1\otimes\Gamma(\phi_2)$ contains $\mathrm{A}_1$? | $J$ non-zero? |
|---|---|---|
| (a) $\Psi_{d1},\Psi_s$ | No | **No** |
| (b) $\Psi_{d1},\Psi_{p1}$ | Yes | **Yes** |
| (c) $\Psi_{d2},\Psi_{d1}$ | No | **No** |
| (d) $\Psi_s,\Psi_{d3}$ | No | **No** |
| (e) $\Psi_{p1},\Psi_s$ | Yes | **Yes** |

The dipole matrix element $J$ can be non-zero only for pairs **(b)** and **(e)**; it vanishes by symmetry for pairs **(a)**, **(c)**, and **(d)**.

